@Alex:
to me, Terracotta appears to take the way the other way around: instantiate the application software for each node (or process, the unit of failure anyways), but then distribute the instances of the domain model objects over a set of nodes. Which I think is very worthwhile, but still somehow the other way around ;-)

I should read up on Terracotta a bit, I'm interested in how you guys handle application failure (and also the whole thing seems to be very cool in general).

@mbien:
I've been there. But Scala doesn't really solve the problems Erik's and my article were about. The actors implementation there is cool, but I'm still sceptical if Scala doesn't add too much complexity for the benefits it brings.
